ee (H10-I9-J8)\n- I10: creates vertical open three (I8-I9-I10) and extends horizontal pair (H10-I10)\n\nI10 seems better because it strengthens both vertical and horizontal possibilities.\n\nBut is there a move that creates a **double threat**?\n\nWait — what about **J9**?\n\nJ9: currently empty.\n\nIf White plays J9:\n- Diagonally: I9=O, J9=? — not aligned with others.\n- Vertically: J9 alone.\n- Horizontally: I9-J9 → pair.\n- Diagonal (down-right): H10-I9-J9? H10 to I9 is (row--, col++), I9 to J9 is (row--, col++)? I9 is row9, J9 is row9 — no, same row.\n\nWait: H10 (row10,H), I9 (row9,I), J8 (row8,J) — that’s one diagonal.\n\nH10 (row10,H), I10 (row10,I), J10 (row10,J) — horizontal.\n\nI8 (row8,I), I9 (row9,I), I10 (row10,I) — vertical.\n\nNo direct alignment for J9 with existing O stones.\n\nSo J9 is weak.\n\nBack to core.\n\nAnother idea: can White play **G9**?\n\nG9: empty.\n\nG9 adjacent to H9=X — but White has no nearby stones. I9=O, but G9 to I9 is two steps — not helpful.\n\nSo not promising.\n\nBest candidates:\n1. **J8** — completes diagonal three: H10-I9-J8\n2. **I10** — completes vertical three: I8-I9-I10, and starts horizontal: H10-I10\n3. **J10** — extends horizontal: H10-I10-J10? But I10 not occupied yet.\n\nI10 seems best because:\n- It creates a vertical open three in column I (I8, I9, I10), which can grow to four with I11 or I7.\n- It also forms a horizontal pair with H10, potentially leading to H10-I10-J10-K10-L10-M10.\n- This gives White two directions of expansion.\n\nMoreover, it blocks any Black attempt to use I10 for their own patterns.\n\nBut is there a more urgent defensive need?\n\nCheck: does Black have a lethal threat?\n\nBlack has:\n- H7, H8, H9 — three vertical. To make four, needs H6 or H10. H10 is O — blocked. So only H6 possible. So H6 is a potential threat square, but not immediate — White doesn\'t need to block H6 now.\n\nBlack diagonal: G8, H9, I10 — G8=X, H9=X, I10=empty. So if Black plays I10, they get three in diagonal: G8-H9-I10. Then could extend to J11, etc.\n\nSo I10 is a shared threat point.\n\nTherefore, **playing I10 prevents Black from starting that diagonal**.\n\nThus, I10 serves dual purpose:\n- Offensive: builds vertical three for White\n- Defensive: blocks Black’s diagonal start\n\nAdditionally, White’s vertical in I-column becomes strong.\n\nTherefore, I10 is a high-value move.\n\nNow, is there any move where White can create an **immediate win**? No — no four-in-a-row possible yet.\n\nBut among candidate moves, I10 stands out.\n\nWait — what about **J8** vs **I10**?\n\nJ8 only helps diagonal, which is less developed.\n\nI10 strengthens a vertical line that already has two stones (I8, I9) — adding third.\n\nAlso, note that I7 is empty — so future I7 move would make four.\n\nMoreover, after I10, if Black doesn’t respond, White can play I11 next, making four.\n\nBut Black may respond.\n\nStill, I10 is better than J8.\n\nAnother option: **H11**?\n\nH11 is below H10. H10=O, H11=empty.\n\nPlaying H11 would make vertical two in H: H10-H11, but H9=X — so cannot connect upward. So only downward — weak.\n\nNot good.\n\nThus, best move is **I10**.\n\nBut is I10 unoccupied?\n\nFrom board:\n- Row 10: A10 to O10 — only H10 has O. I10 is empty.\n- Yes.\n\nAnd not in occupied list: given occupied: H7,G8,H8,I8,H9,I9,H10 — I10 not listed.\n\nSo valid.\n\nTherefore:\n\nEvaluation: I10 is a strategic point that allows White to build a vertical three in column I while simultaneously blocking Black’s potential diagonal (G8-H9-I10). Other moves like J8 offer growth but with less immediate impact or defensive value.\n\nReason: Playing I10 maximizes offensive potential by creating an open three with extension paths both up and down the column, while denying Black a key development square. It balances attack and defense optimally.\n\nAnswer: {"col": "I", "row": 10}'}
